WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:03.970
Some movies ask a little bit more of the audience
than others.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:08.460
It's easy to mindlessly enjoy the latest big-budget
explosion-fest, but that doesn't work as well

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.000
with films that require real brain power.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.070
In some cases, unconventional narrative structures
and gaping plot holes require audiences to

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.790
help piece together the puzzle after the credits
roll.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:23.810
In the case of these films, it's those fan
theories that truly provide the missing links.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.480
Grease's flying car

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.230
Grease doesn't exactly thrive on realism;
people in the '50s didn't really burst into

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.430
song while fixing up hot rods and hosting
slumber parties.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.070
That's strange enough, but by the end of the
film, things get even weirder when Danny and

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:41.370
Sandy hop into a shining red convertible and
ride into the clouds.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.420
Viewers might be able to suspend disbelief
that all these 20- and 30-something actors

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.510
were still in high school, but flying cars
at the fair?

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.510
No way.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.530
But if you listen closely, there might be
a more realistic explanation for all of the

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.690
movie's eccentricities: Sandy isn't actually
alive.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.290
In moment of their lyrical recap of their
supposed summer love story, Danny tell us:

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.540
"I saved her life.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.239
She nearly drowned."

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.310
But what if he didn't save her life after
all and this is all just some elaborate fever

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.420
dream Sandy's having before she reaches the
other side?

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.470
It's not hard to believe that she'd invent
some crazy love story in her oxygen-deprived

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.180
mind before passing over to the great beyond,
with the climax being her literal ascent into

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.180
heaven.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.090
The theory doesn't just explain Grease; it
also helps shed some light on its ill-advised

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.090
sequel.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.960
After all, if Grease is a happy dream, then
Grease 2 is most likely a nightmare—at least

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.010
for anyone who's watching it.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.880
The Goonies' country club cover up

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.340
As classic as The Goonies is, it's hard to
believe that the architects of Astoria, Oregon

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.229
didn't happen upon all those man-made underground
tunnels and treasure when they were building

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.229
the town.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:48.480
Sure, it makes for a good adventure for the
kiddos who discover them during their quest

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.119
to save the city from greedy developers, but
they couldn't be the first people in a hundred

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:53.880
years to set foot in there, right?

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.799
But one theory posits that the wealthy real
estate developers who want to take over know

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:02.189
about the treasure below… and they're actively
plundering One-Eyed Willie's stash in order

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:04.049
to fund their decadent lifestyle.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.950
The country club isn't really the end goal.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.769
It's a cover-up, designed to keep the underground
treasure trove safe.

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:11.689
Willy Wonka's secret ingredient

00:02:11.689 --> 00:02:15.840
Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory might
end on a high note, but for most, the Golden

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.480
Ticket is no prize at all.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.379
Throughout the film, four of the five children
who've been invited to tour the factory wind

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:26.099
up gone, possibly disfigured, and never heard
from again, as their parents are carted away

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:27.790
by creepy rhyming minions.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.790
"The squeezing!

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.019
She has to be squeezed immediately before
she explodes!"

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:36.489
So, what if Wonka is actually just a serial
child-abductor who relies on the flesh of

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.810
these kids to make his recipes complete?

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.700
After all, he does lead these little ones
through rooms designed to exploit their specific

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:43.700
weaknesses.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.810
The Oompa-loompas have complex pre-planned
song and dance routines that reference individual

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.169
children by name.

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:54.120
Not to mention, all of the pipes in the Chocolate
Factory are suspiciously child-sized.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.870
If children are the secret ingredient in Wonka's
candy, the reason Charlie survives isn't because

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.419
he's such a great kid—it's because he's
the only one desperate enough to continue

00:03:02.419 --> 00:03:06.650
Wonka's work, especially now that he's seen
exactly what it takes to lure his peers to

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.809
their sugar-coated doom.

00:03:08.809 --> 00:03:10.279
No aliens allowed

00:03:10.279 --> 00:03:14.620
After The Sixth Sense and Unbreakable, director
and writer M. Night Shyamalan became famous

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.040
for his films' clever plot twists, but his
next directorial outing, Signs, failed to

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.629
impress a lot of fans.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:24.459
For most of the film, Signs is about a former
pastor who struggles with his faith while

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:27.219
defending his family against invading aliens.

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:31.049
As a thriller, Signs is well-paced and exciting—until
the twist.

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:35.260
As it turns out, Signs' aliens can be defeated
by water, leaving the audience to wonder why

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.239
a supposedly intelligent lifeform would try
to conquer a planet that's 71 percent covered

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:41.340
by the stuff.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.439
Unless, as some fans argue, the aliens aren't
aliens at all.

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:46.439
They're demons.

00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:47.439
Think about it.

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:51.109
Signs' various characters interpret the creatures'
true identities in different ways -- to the

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:55.219
sheriff, they're just mischievous kids, while
an officer in the Army thinks they're an invading

00:03:55.219 --> 00:03:56.540
military force.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.930
But the "aliens" don't have any technology—in
fact, they can't even open doors.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.359
As such, the creatures aren't weak to water.

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:04.889
They're weak to holy water.

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:09.299
Mel Gibson's daughter, who has the irritating
habit of leaving half-empty glasses of water

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:13.040
scattered around the house, is described at
various points as an angel.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.249
That might be literal.

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:17.920
Since she poured it, the water that she leaves
lying around is blessed, and provides the

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.950
key to the demons' destruction.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.600
Thematically, demons make a lot more sense
than aliens since the main character arc is

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.970
about a spiritual reawakening, which makes
the movie so much more than just sci-fi schlock.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.410
Someone else's totem

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.250
The ending of Inception will be the topic
of debate for decades to come.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.310
Throughout the film, a corporate spy named
Cobb appears to use a spinning top in order

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.250
to separate fiction from reality.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.480
If the top falls over, it means that Cobb
is in the real world.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.650
If the totem spins forever, he's dreaming.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.720
At the end of the movie, Cobb spins the top,
but wanders away before he sees if it falls

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.720
or not.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.430
Most people assume that this means that Inception's
ending is ambiguous—but maybe it's not.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.850
Maybe his totem isn't the top but his wedding
ring, which he's always wearing in the dream

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.150
world, but never in the real one.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.590
After all, a totem is supposed to be personal
and never shared, and Cobb never explicitly

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.280
says the top is his totem.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.850
Both were also given to him by his late wife.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.160
He doesn't appear to be wearing his ring in
the film's last scene, so if you're a sucker

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.450
for happy endings, this might just be the
fan theory for you.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.450
Thanks for watching!

